<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gerhard III, Count of Holstein-Rendsburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1292–<a href="/years/1340">1340</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Gerhard the Great, Gerhard the Bald</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gerhard III (c. 1292–1340), called the Great or the Bald, was Count of Holstein-Rendsburg and the dominant power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> during the interregnum of 1332–1340. After defeating King Christopher II he held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> and Funen in pledge and controlled the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> as guardian of its young duke. He was murdered at Randers in 1340 by the Jutish nobleman Niels Ebbesen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gerhard-iii-count-of-holstein-rendsburg.jpg" alt="Gerhard III, Count of Holstein-Rendsburg" width="320" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thiset's book contains contributions from a number of artists. This illustration (seal no. 164) was drawn by Emmery Rondahl (1858-1914). … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grevgerhardssegl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gerhard belonged to the Schauenburg family and was the son of Henry I, Count of Holstein-Rendsburg, who died on 5 August 1304. He inherited his share of Holstein as a boy and, as a young man, enlarged it by outmanoeuvring his relatives and conquering other parts of the county, which made him a powerful local prince. He also fought as a paid condottiere for neighbouring kings, among them King Eric VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, often together with his cousin John III, Count of Holstein-Plön. He married Sophia of Werle, a granddaughter of Eric V of Denmark, and their children included Henry II and Nicholas, later counts of Holstein-Rendsburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1325 Gerhard became guardian of his young nephew, Duke Valdemar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a>, which made him a natural ally of the discontented Danish magnates. In 1326 he and the rebels deposed Christopher II, placed Valdemar on the Danish throne and made Gerhard regent. Through the Constitutio Valdemariana he secured the separation of Schleswig from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, the basis of lasting German influence in South Jutland. When rebellions and a quarrel with Count Johan followed, he restored Christopher as a puppet king in 1329, and after Christopher's death in 1332 he ruled Jutland and Funen, leaving the rest of the country to Johan.</p><p>His hold was insecure. Creditors who had paid for his armies could press him, the Danish gentry that had once backed him turned against foreign rule, peasant risings and lawlessness at sea spread, and neighbouring German states supported Christopher's son, the future Valdemar IV. Gerhard seems to have been ready to compromise if his debts were settled, but in spring 1340 he set out against rebels in North Jutland. On 1 April 1340, at Randers, Niels Ebbesen and some of his men entered his bedroom and killed him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His death ended Holstein rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. His sons gave up his Danish titles, and Valdemar IV took the throne. In Denmark Gerhard has traditionally been seen as a German tyrant, and his killing as a high point of the nation's history that still inspired poets and writers during the German occupation of 1940–1945. He is also remembered unfavourably in Dithmarschen, which he ravaged, while in Holstein he has been regarded as a national hero. Contemporary sources show him as energetic, a brutal warrior and deeply religious.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gerhard III of Holstein-Rendsburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gerhard III the Great of Holstein-Rendsburg</b> dominated Denmark in the 1330s, ruling Jutland until his murder in 1340.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-holstein">County of Holstein</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Randers, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.46,10.04&amp;year=1323" aria-label="Randers on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4767 244 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Gerhard III, Count of Holstein-Rendsburg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5217 514h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5217 514h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=56.46,10.04&amp;year=1323">What was here in 1323? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
